INT. SALT-PORK STORAGE LOFT, SHIPYARD - EVENING, 1840s

A low-beamed room smelling of tar and brine. Barrels line the walls. A single oil lamp casts a shaky circle on the planks. Rain drums the roof.

Six figures sit on barrel tops around a makeshift table of planks.

CAPTAIN EZEKIAL HOLLOWAY (50s, weathered, grey queue) presides. Beside him is his first mate, SILAS PETHERBRIDGE (30s, red whiskers, cheerful). Across from them sits the shipwright, OLUWASEUN ADEBAYO (40s, broad-shouldered, leather apron). Next to him, the ship's cook, MRS. ABIGAIL FINCH (60s, spectacles, sleeves rolled). At the end, young cabin boy JEREMIAH TUCKER (13, freckled, wide-eyed). Lastly, the agent, MR. THEODORE VANE (40s, black coat, ink-stained fingers) sits stiffly apart.

HOLLOWAY
Gentlemen. Madam. We sail Thursday if the weather holds.

PETHERBRIDGE
It won't, Captain.

HOLLOWAY
It will if I will it so.

ADEBAYO
The hull will hold. The cargo is another question.

VANE
The cargo is insured.

FINCH
Insured against what, Mr. Vane? A woman cannot pickle a drowned ship.

VANE
Against everything, Mrs. Finch.

PETHERBRIDGE
Except honesty.

JEREMIAH
Sir, what is the cargo?

Silence. Vane looks to Holloway. Holloway looks at his hands.

HOLLOWAY
Tea, boy.

JEREMIAH
Only tea?

ADEBAYO
(gently)
Tea and what comes with it.

VANE
Mr. Adebayo, I would remind you of your contract.

ADEBAYO
I made the contract in good faith. I did not know the ship was meant for Lisbon.

FINCH
Lisbon.

VANE
A port like any other.

PETHERBRIDGE
Not for what we carry.

HOLLOWAY
Enough.

A long pause. Rain on the roof.

JEREMIAH
I don't mind, sir. I can keep quiet.

FINCH
(softly)
That, child, is exactly what worries me.
